WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:11.800 --> 00:00:13.342
Good afternoon, everyone.

00:00:13.342 --> 00:00:14.550
Thank you all for being here.

00:00:14.550 --> 00:00:16.840
Thank you, Ed, and
everyone who's contributed

00:00:16.840 --> 00:00:19.230
to creating this space for us.

00:00:19.230 --> 00:00:20.850
My name is Kester
Barrow, and I'm

00:00:20.850 --> 00:00:23.560
an area director for MacGregor
House and residential life

00:00:23.560 --> 00:00:26.390
programs here at MIT.

00:00:26.390 --> 00:00:28.410
I'm both anxious
and excited today

00:00:28.410 --> 00:00:31.330
to really engage in this
conversation, this dialogue,

00:00:31.330 --> 00:00:33.850
with you all.

00:00:33.850 --> 00:00:36.610
At the beginning of the
spring 2016 semester,

00:00:36.610 --> 00:00:38.580
the MIT community
received an announcement

00:00:38.580 --> 00:00:40.650
from President Reif,
sharing with us

00:00:40.650 --> 00:00:42.990
a tremendous
scientific discovery.

00:00:42.990 --> 00:00:46.540
He shared that in collaboration
with Caltech and the National

00:00:46.540 --> 00:00:48.920
Science Foundation,
researchers at MIT

00:00:48.920 --> 00:00:51.310
had recorded the
first direct detection

00:00:51.310 --> 00:00:52.940
of gravitational waves.

00:00:52.940 --> 00:00:55.590
He stated, "I do not
typically write to you

00:00:55.590 --> 00:00:58.540
to celebrate individual
research achievements

00:00:58.540 --> 00:01:00.230
no matter how impressive.

00:01:00.230 --> 00:01:03.320
Our community produces
important work all the time,

00:01:03.320 --> 00:01:05.489
but I urge you to reflect
on today's announcement

00:01:05.489 --> 00:01:08.060
because it demonstrates
on a grand scale

00:01:08.060 --> 00:01:11.940
why and how human beings pursue
deep scientific questions

00:01:11.940 --> 00:01:14.280
and why it matters."

00:01:14.280 --> 00:01:16.930
Today, given President
Reif's words,

00:01:16.930 --> 00:01:19.750
please keep these two
following challenges in mind

00:01:19.750 --> 00:01:22.070
as we engage with each
other throughout today's

00:01:22.070 --> 00:01:23.540
conversation.

00:01:23.540 --> 00:01:27.000
I challenge you to pursue
deep social questions.

00:01:27.000 --> 00:01:29.680
I challenge you to
define for yourself

00:01:29.680 --> 00:01:33.910
why does understanding
these social issues matter?

00:01:33.910 --> 00:01:36.050
Race and other socially
constructed identities

00:01:36.050 --> 00:01:37.270
aren't real.

00:01:37.270 --> 00:01:39.130
They're tools created
by the oppressors

00:01:39.130 --> 00:01:43.140
to maintain the social norms at
the expense of the oppressed;

00:01:43.140 --> 00:01:46.420
however, race is a lived
experience for us all

00:01:46.420 --> 00:01:48.510
that's been part of
the fabric of the USA

00:01:48.510 --> 00:01:50.840
since the founding
of this nation.

00:01:50.840 --> 00:01:54.550
We cannot negate the harm
and the pain that we've all

00:01:54.550 --> 00:01:58.380
experienced as a result
of this oppressive system.

00:01:58.380 --> 00:02:01.150
If we truly seek to
achieve change and growth,

00:02:01.150 --> 00:02:04.330
we must embrace the challenge
of acknowledging our history

00:02:04.330 --> 00:02:06.170
and seeking change.

00:02:06.170 --> 00:02:09.520
By embracing these challenges,
we accept the shame.

00:02:09.520 --> 00:02:11.940
We will be tempted to
shy away from the shame.

00:02:11.940 --> 00:02:14.990
Some of us will retreat
inwards and attack ourselves

00:02:14.990 --> 00:02:18.030
based upon that shame, others
will project their shame

00:02:18.030 --> 00:02:20.920
outwards and attack others.

00:02:20.920 --> 00:02:23.250
However, please
keep this in mind.

00:02:23.250 --> 00:02:25.620
As a man, it is
my responsibility

00:02:25.620 --> 00:02:27.580
to process my
shame around gender

00:02:27.580 --> 00:02:29.920
if I truly seek gender equity.

00:02:29.920 --> 00:02:32.230
As a cisgendered
heterosexual male,

00:02:32.230 --> 00:02:35.860
is my responsibility to process
my shame around sexuality

00:02:35.860 --> 00:02:38.810
if I truly seek to support
the movement for equity

00:02:38.810 --> 00:02:41.530
for the queer community.

00:02:41.530 --> 00:02:44.100
Members of our community are
already gathering in groups

00:02:44.100 --> 00:02:46.500
to support each other in
a processing of shared

00:02:46.500 --> 00:02:50.110
identities and the emotions that
stem from those conversations.

00:02:50.110 --> 00:02:53.050
In the coming months, it is
the hope of many in this room

00:02:53.050 --> 00:02:56.130
that these spaces
become a norm at MIT.

00:02:56.130 --> 00:02:59.430
Where the purpose isn't
to segregate, but instead

00:02:59.430 --> 00:03:03.150
to achieve insight and
foster support and healing.

00:03:03.150 --> 00:03:05.890
If our aim is to
truly pursue change,

00:03:05.890 --> 00:03:08.840
we must try new strategies
because the current strategies

00:03:08.840 --> 00:03:11.830
have only taken us thus far.

00:03:11.830 --> 00:03:14.030
In closing, I'd like
to take a moment

00:03:14.030 --> 00:03:16.040
to acknowledge the weight
of the violence that's

00:03:16.040 --> 00:03:19.300
plagued our society
over the last few weeks.

00:03:19.300 --> 00:03:22.330
Alton Sterling, you matter.

00:03:22.330 --> 00:03:25.620
Philando Castile, you matter.

00:03:25.620 --> 00:03:29.460
The five officers who died
in Dallas, you matter.

00:03:29.460 --> 00:03:33.570
The 49 souls lost in
Orlando, you matter.

00:03:33.570 --> 00:03:37.010
The victims of the bombings in
Turkey, Bangladesh, and Iraq,

00:03:37.010 --> 00:03:38.595
you matter.

00:03:38.595 --> 00:03:42.790
Members of the MIT community
who don't feel safe, you matter.

00:03:42.790 --> 00:03:44.220
Thank you.

00:03:44.220 --> 00:03:47.270
[APPLAUSE]

